I can't claim to have any normative definition. But it's the many design
issues that reveal _advantages vs. drawbacks_ of decisions you make in the
game, and all the _non-static situations_ that you cannot perfectly rely
on (you know what I mean?), and last but not least the _probabilities of
monster and object generation_, and the _tuned interaction of objects_.
The game is in so many respects thoughtfully tuned that even if there are
(especially for the advanced players in the late game) a few issues that
seem (or are) unbalanced the game is overall very well balanced; or call
it tuned - the game features are tuned against each other (as far as that
is possible in a complex game) so that most interactions don't provide
extreem situation (like too easy or too difficult play). Do you have some
good definition, maybe?

